WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.206
Today is October 09, 2026.

3
00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:15.111
Overnight, Wall Street weathered a sharp divergence across asset classes as macro volatility collided with tech balance sheets.

4
00:00:15.111 --> 00:00:19.130
The tech-heavy Nasdaq dropped over one and a quarter percent.

5
00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:25.344
The S and P 500 slipped forty-seven hundredths of a percent, pinned beneath seventy-eight hundred.

6
00:00:25.344 --> 00:00:31.152
The Dow Jones held relatively steady as cyclical value absorbed institutional rotation.

7
00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:34.143
Market breadth deteriorated sharply.

8
00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:40.552
Decliners overwhelmed gainers nearly three to one on the Nasdaq, signaling underlying fragility.

9
00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:45.307
In fixed income, benchmark yields showed early signs of plateauing.

10
00:00:45.307 --> 00:00:49.645
The ten-year Treasury yield hovered around five point two percent.

11
00:00:49.645 --> 00:00:55.871
The thirty-year yield printed at five point six percent, levels not seen in over two decades.

12
00:00:55.871 --> 00:01:01.177
A massive sixty-billion-dollar Treasury buyback met robust investor demand.

13
00:01:01.177 --> 00:01:05.711
Meanwhile, geopolitical whiplash dominated the oil patch.

14
00:01:05.711 --> 00:01:12.464
Brent crude spiked toward one hundred five dollars on renewed Middle East headlines before paring half its gains.

15
00:01:12.464 --> 00:01:19.731
Hurricane Isaiah shut down over sixty percent of Gulf of Mexico oil production, keeping energy floors firm.

16
00:01:19.731 --> 00:01:27.978
On monetary policy, Fed officials signaled that October remains a hold, but markets are pricing rising odds of a December rate hike.

17
00:01:27.978 --> 00:01:32.648
The macro debate has shifted from rate peak levels to terminal duration.

18
00:01:32.648 --> 00:01:40.050
This macro liquidity pinch triggered an aggressive reshuffling across technology and artificial intelligence supply chains.

19
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:47.464
Institutional desks used negative headlines around OpenAI annualized revenues as a pre-earnings excuse to rotate.

20
00:01:47.464 --> 00:01:54.657
FT reported OpenAI hit fifty billion in run-rate revenue, missing whispered media targets of seventy billion.

21
00:01:54.657 --> 00:02:01.643
In reality, differing revenue accounting between direct cloud partnerships and platform sales drove the variance.

22
00:02:01.643 --> 00:02:08.187
Even so, high-beta hardware, optical interconnects, and data center power took an immediate hit.

23
00:02:08.187 --> 00:02:12.353
Applied Optoelectronics plummeted over thirteen percent.

24
00:02:12.353 --> 00:02:19.302
Power producers Constellation Energy and Vistra saw intraday pullbacks as grid power bottlenecks made headlines.

25
00:02:19.302 --> 00:02:22.476
Yet fundamental demand remains blistering.

26
00:02:22.476 --> 00:02:30.785
Taiwan Semiconductor posted September revenues of sixteen billion dollars, with third-quarter sales up fifty-one percent year over year.

27
00:02:30.785 --> 00:02:38.701
Capital flows are clearly taking profits from expensive AI hardware to hide in yield-sensitive cyclicals and cash equivalents.

28
00:02:38.701 --> 00:02:45.748
Retail and consumer names like Home Depot, PepsiCo, and McDonald's soaked up defensive bid flows.

29
00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:50.025
Turning to our pivotal single-stock deep dives for the trading day.

30
00:02:50.025 --> 00:02:54.584
First, Nvidia and the AI infrastructure proxy basket.

31
00:02:54.584 --> 00:02:58.199
Fundamental earnings contribution remains staggering.

32
00:02:58.199 --> 00:03:04.547
AI hardware suppliers now account for over half of total S and P five hundred earnings growth.

33
00:03:04.547 --> 00:03:09.192
However, micro headwinds are surfacing in working capital metrics.

34
00:03:09.192 --> 00:03:18.285
Accounts receivable turnover slowed over fifteen percent quarter over quarter, signaling stretched downstream liquidity and longer client payment cycles.

35
00:03:18.285 --> 00:03:27.108
On the charts, the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index pulled back to twelve thousand four hundred, a former resistance now acting as primary support.

36
00:03:27.108 --> 00:03:33.125
The triple-Q tested its ten-day moving average and closed at seven hundred forty-six point seven.

37
00:03:33.125 --> 00:03:37.157
Holding this prior breakout zone validates the bull trend.

38
00:03:37.157 --> 00:03:42.304
A clean break below warns of a false breakout and invites broader liquidation.

39
00:03:42.416 --> 00:03:45.356
Second, Micron Technology.

40
00:03:45.306 --> 00:03:51.617
Micron closed around ten hundred fifty dollars, sparking an intense thesis clash on Wall Street.

41
00:03:51.617 --> 00:03:58.002
Conservative desks view the stock as a late-cycle memory play capped at single-digit earnings multiples.

42
00:03:58.002 --> 00:04:05.477
Bulls argue that AI High Bandwidth Memory and long-term Strategic Customer Agreements are rewriting industry cyclicality.

43
00:04:05.477 --> 00:04:11.691
Bull-case models project fiscal twenty-twenty-seven earnings of one hundred fifty-eight dollars per share.

44
00:04:11.691 --> 00:04:17.058
Paired with a nineteen-times growth multiple, that implies a three-thousand-dollar target.

45
00:04:17.058 --> 00:04:24.362
Near-term risks include potential Taiwan fabrication labor strikes and hyperscaler capital expenditure moderation.

46
00:04:24.362 --> 00:04:38.198
Tactical execution demands strict risk tiers: initiate twenty-five percent sizing between eight hundred ninety-five and one thousand dollars, thirty-five percent between eight and nine hundred, and forty percent between seven and eight hundred.

47
00:04:38.198 --> 00:04:43.921
A structural close below seven hundred dollars serves as the absolute stop-loss boundary.

48
00:04:43.921 --> 00:04:46.617
Third, Meta Platforms.

49
00:04:46.617 --> 00:04:53.651
Fresh regulatory disclosures reveal substantial institutional front-running and high-conviction insider accumulation.

50
00:04:53.651 --> 00:05:00.171
Meta remains a dual beneficiary as an AI agent operating system and enterprise model distributor.

51
00:05:00.171 --> 00:05:07.266
Key technical support sits firm at its late-summer breakout floor, with resistance clustered near all-time highs.

52
00:05:07.266 --> 00:05:12.377
Maintain position discipline as October volatility tests sector multiples.

53
00:05:12.377 --> 00:05:16.384
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

54
00:05:16.384 --> 00:05:20.551
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
